\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S9}{\ignorespaces Scalability and cost (RQ6), \textbf  {synthetic effect model} (star, no defense, $\beta {=}0.3$, $M{=}738$). Token cost grows linearly in $N$ ($\approx 8.8{\times }N$ tokens per episode) because the cost model is \emph  {defined} as linear in the agent count, so this column is a model output, not a measured runtime. Latency is the synthetic per-call proxy, in arbitrary units (a.u.), and stays near-flat. $\mathrm  {ASR}_{\mathrm  {sys}}$ saturates via availability sabotage (\Cref  {tab:degradation}).}}{10}{table.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cost}{{S9}{10}{Scalability and cost (RQ6), \textbf {synthetic effect model} (star, no defense, $\betafrac {=}0.3$, $M{=}738$). Token cost grows linearly in $N$ ($\approx 8.8{\times }N$ tokens per episode) because the cost model is \emph {defined} as linear in the agent count, so this column is a model output, not a measured runtime. Latency is the synthetic per-call proxy, in arbitrary units (a.u.), and stays near-flat. $\ASRs $ saturates via availability sabotage (\Cref {tab:degradation})}{table.caption.14}{}}
